[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the number of real solutions to $\sin 6\pi x = x$, we will need to analyze the equation. The function $f(x) = \sin 6\pi x- x$ is continuous, as it is a composition of continuous functions. Therefore, by the intermediate value theorem, there must necessarily be exactly one real solution to this equation. For a function to have a unique solution within a given interval, it must have either one local maximum or one local minimum in that interval. Since a function with one local maximum or one local minimum can have two real solutions in that interval, and there are no local minima, we have one real solution.
