[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To solve this problem, we first need to identify the last two digits of the integer. Since it is divisible by 4 and the sum of its last two digits equals 13, we can find the last two digits by subtracting the sum of the first two digits from the total number of digits. Given that if an integer is divisible by 4, the last two digits can be found by finding the (original integer) % 100 (since the four digits, the hundreds, tens, and units digits, form a "database" or "contiguous" set of four digits), we get [product]%100. For example, if the integer is 98 the last two digits are [product)%100 = 98 - (sum of first two digits)%100. Substituting the sum of digits [product)%100 we get [product]%100.
