[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m How many degrees are in the measure of the smaller angle that is formed by the hour-hand and minute-hand of a clock when it is 5 o'clock?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the smaller angle formed by the hour hand and minute hand of a clock at 5 o'clock, we should first calculate the degrees of these hands in terms of their regular movements.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 1. The clock is divided into 12 hours of 30 minutes each, so there are 540 minute hours between a 12-hour cycle.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2. The clock shows a 5 in the bottom right of the hour face. Since these two hands move together, their absolute difference over one complete revolution will be the absolute difference between the hour and minute hands.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 3. With 12 hour hands and an interlocking minute part, the 5 will be moving on a minute hand, resulting in 5 divisions of 60 minutes (or one hour).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 4. When the clock is 5 o'clock, the minute hand has moved 30 minutes (one hour) and started from the 12 o'clock position, completing one revolution in 60 minutes.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 5. Since 1 hour has three 60-minute divisions, 30 minutes will bring us back to a sixth of a revolution from the 12 o'clock position.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
