[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The horizontal asymptote is $y = 2,$ and the only vertical asymptote is $x = -2.$  Find $\frac{p(3)}{q(3)}.$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Given that the horizontal asymptote is $y = 2$ and the vertical asymptote is $x = -2,$ we know that $q(3)$ has a factor of $(x + 2),$ leaving the $y$-intercept of the graph unchanged. This congruence implies $f(3) = q(3) = 8.$ Given that $q(x) = (x + 2)(x - 4),$ $p(x)$ can be found using the linearity and disjoint nature. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We write $p(x)$ as $f_1(x) = g_1(x) + g_3(x),$ where $g_3(x) = (x - 4)(x + 2).$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find $f(3),$ we plug $x = 3$ into the two equations. Thus, 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$f(3) = g_1(3) + g_3(3) = f_1(3) + f_3(3).$$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Letting 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$f_1 = 8, \quad g_3 = 4 $$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m and 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$f_3 = 0,$$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m we find $f(3) = 8 + 4 = 12.$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\boxed{12}$<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \frac{4}{5}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
