[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since Nikki has 25 roses, we can write the ratio as:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Roses / Roses (in ratio terms) = irises / Roses (in ratio terms)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Or, 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 25 / Roses (in ratio terms) = irises / Roses (in ratio terms)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We can now rewrite this equation to solve for irises:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m (25 / Roses) * (2 / 5) = irises / Roses
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m irises = (25 * 2 / 5) * Roses
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m irises = 10 * Roses
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now we know that Nikki has 10 irises.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2. On the other hand, Nikki is adding 20 more roses, so she will now have 25 + 20 = 45 roses. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We can now use the same ratio of irises to roses (2:5) to find out how many irises she must have.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m irises / 25 roses (in ratio terms) = irises / 45 roses (in ratio terms)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m irises = (25 * 2) / 45
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m irises = 2 * 2 / 45
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m irises = 4 / 45
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So, after adding 20 more roses, Nikki will have 4 irises in total.
