[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m For a finite sequence $A=(a_1,a_2,\dots,a_n)$ of numbers, the Cesaro sum of $A$ is defined to be
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\frac{S_1+\cdots+S_n}{n},\]where $S_k=a_1+\cdots+a_k$ and $1\leq k\leq n$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m If the Cesaro sum of the 99-term sequence $(a_1,\dots,a_{99})$ is 1000,  what is the Cesaro sum of the 100-term sequence $(1,a_1,\dots,a_{99})$?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$S_k = \begin{cases} \frac{S_1}{1} & \text{if } k = 1 \\ \frac{S_{k-1} + a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n}{k} & \text{if } k > 1 \end{cases}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$\begin{cases} S_1 +1000 & \text{if } k = 1 \\ \frac{S_{1},a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n}{1} + a_1 & \text{if } k = 2 \\ \frac{S_2 + a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + \cdots + a_n}{2} + a_1 + a_2 & \text{if } k = 3 \\ \frac{S_3 + a_1 + a_2 + a_3 + a_4 + \cdots + a_n}{3} + a_1 + a_2 + a_3 & \text{if } k = 4 \\ \vdots \\ \frac{S_n + a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_n}{n} + a_1 + a_2 + \cdots + a_{n-1} & \text{if } k = n\end{cases}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore,$$
