[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m One caterer charges a basic fee of $\$100$ plus $\$15$ per person. A second caterer charges a basic fee of $\$200$ plus $\$12$ per person. What is the least number of people for which the second caterer is cheaper?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We are solving for $n$, the smallest number of meals where the second caterer is cheaper.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We want to minimize the particular element in the expression
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\dfrac{ n × ~\$ 15~ }{~\$ 100~ }} {~\$ 200~ }$ .
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m This can be written as
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\begin{align} \dfrac{ n × ~\$ 15~ }{~\$ 100~ }} {~\$ 200~ } &= \dfrac{ •~\$ 15 • ~n }~ \$ 100 } { ~ \$ 200 ~ } \\ &= \dfrac{ \$ 195 ~ • \$ n }~ \$ 100000 ~ }{ ~ \$ 200 ~ }=\dfrac{ • ~\$ 195 • ~n }~ ,{~\$ 100000 ~ }}{{~\$ 200 ~ }} \\ &= \dfrac{ \$ 195 ~ • ~n ~ }~ .{~\$ 50 • ~n ~ }}{~ \$ 100000 ~ }}{~ \$ 200 ~ } = \dfrac{ \$ 195 ~ • ~n ~ }~ .{~\$ 50 • ~n ~ }}{~ \$ 100000 ~ }}{~ \$ 200} \\ &= \dfrac{ \$ 5 \cdot \$ 39 \cdot ~n ~ }~ .{~\$ 50 • ~n ~ }}{~ \$ 100000 ~ }}{~ \$ 200}=\dfrac{ \$ 5 \cdot \$ 39 • ~n ~ }{•~\$ 250 • ~n ~ }}{~\$ 100000 ~ }}{~ \$ 200 ~ } =\dfrac{ \$ 5 \cdot \$ 39 • ~n ~ }{ • ~\$ 250 • ~n ~ }} $$<|endoftext|>
