[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m In a certain base $b$, the square of $22_b$ is $514_b$.  What is $b$?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Let us first look separately at  \frac{514_b}{22_b}.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{aligned} \text{Split} &\quad \qquad \text{Sign}\, b &\,& \qquad\,\,\,\,\,\frac{factor}{number}\quad \\ \quad 514 &\quad \quad 22\\ \frac{15}{2} &\quad 12 \\ 15&\quad\quad 12\\ 1\cdot5&\qquad\quad$ 2	
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m  \end{aligned}	
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{aligned} \frac{\color{blue}{\num{1}}\cdot\color{green}{\num{5}}}{\color{green}{\num{11}}}&\,=\,2\,\,	\\ \text{which is the identity solution} &\,\\\end{aligned}	
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m As we can see, this can be expressed as a pair,  \frac{1}{b+1} and 10 \bmod b\,=\, 1. Therefore,  $d=\gcd(b,10,b+1)=b$, according to Bézout's theorem. Thus, we will just have to keep $\bmod b$ throughout to get the final answer.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \boxed{b}
