[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Step 1: Recall that the $\tan$ function calculates the ratio of the opposite side to the adjacent side in a right triangle.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Step 2: Identify the opposite side and adjacent side in the right triangle where the 45-degree angle is located. In a standard right triangle with a 45-degree angle, both sides are equal in length.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Step 3: Since both sides are equal in length, we can represent the adjacent side as $x$ and the opposite side as $x$ as well.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Step 4: Calculate the ratio $\tan 45^\circ$: \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \tan 45^\circ = \frac{\text{opposite}}{\text{adjacent}} = \frac{x}{x}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Step 5: Since the numerator and denominator are the same, we can simplify $\tan 45^\circ$ to: \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \tan 45^\circ = \frac{x}{x} = 1
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
