[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To solve this problem, we first need to calculate the perimeter of each square and then find the positive difference between these perimeters.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 1. **Calculate the perimeter of the first square:**
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - The side length of the square is the width of the figure, which is 5 units.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - The perimeter \( P_1 \) of a square is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m      P_1 = 4 \times \text{side length} = 4 \times 5 = 20 \text{ units}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2. **Calculate the perimeter of the second square:**
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - The side length of the square is the height of the figure, which is 3 units.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - Similarly, the perimeter \( P_2 \) of the second square is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m      P_2 = 4 \times \text{side length} = 4 \times 3 = 12 \text{ units}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 3. **Calculate the positive difference between the perimeters:**
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - The positive difference between \( P_1 \) and \( P_2 \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m      |P_1 - P_2| = |20 - 12| = 8 \text{ units}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Thus, the positive difference between the perimeters of the two figures is \(\boxed{8}\).<|endoftext|>
