[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Let $x=a^2,b^2$ be two integers don't divide each other. Then $100\leq a^2<b^2\leq 200$ for all possible values of $a,b$. By using half-open intervals as such an intervalization is possible. But the set of such $a,b$ is relatively simple and only occurs for a few specific values of $a,b$. The most important idea here is that $a$ and $b$ could be irrational numbers or both. As $a,b$ and hence $x$ can be ever large by adding 100, we could have $x=n+a^2, n\mbox{ is a nonzero integer}$. Then we have $\sqrt{100}+100\leq x\leq \sqrt{200}+100$ which implies that $n\le\sqrt{100}$. Similarly $n+sq\leq \sqrt{200}+100$. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m With this in-body we only have $111$ numbers from which we can interpolate adding $±2$ (55 of them, but by $±200$ arithmetic we can also interpolate).
