[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{Use the identity } \binom{n}{k} = \binom{n}{n-k} \text{ to get: } \binom{15}{10} = \binom{15}{5} = \frac{15!}{5!10!}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{Using the identity } \binom{n}{k} = \binom{n}{n-k}, \text{ we can rewrite the equation as: } \binom{10}{5} + \binom{10}{10} = 10!$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{We know that } \binom{10}{5} = \frac{10!}{5!5!} = \frac{10 \cdot 9 \cdot 8 \cdot 7 \cdot 6}{5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1} = 252$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{Similarly, } \binom{10}{10} = 1$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{This gives}: 252 + 1 = 253. \text{ But this result is incorrect.}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{Re-arranging the earlier equation, we get: } \binom{15}{10} = \binom{15}{5} + \binom{10}{5}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{Using the identity } \binom{n}{k} = \binom{n}{n-k}, \text{ we multiply: } \color{#A0A0A0}{\binom{15}{10} = \binom{15}{5} \cdot \binom{5}{5} + \binom{10}{5} \cdot \binom{5}{5}}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{Since } \binom{5}{5} = 1, \text{ the equation becomes: } \color{#A0A0A0}{\binom{15}{10} = \binom{15}{5} + \binom{10}{5}}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{We know that } \binom{15}{5} = \frac{15!}{5!10!} = \frac{15 \cdot 14 \cdot 13 \cdot 12 \cdot 11}{5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1} = 3003$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{We also know that } \binom{10}{5} = 252$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{This gives the equation: } \color{#A0A0A0}{\binom{15}{10} = 3003 + 252}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{And: } \binom{15}{10} = 3255. \text{ Therefore, the answer is }$.boxed{\color{#A0A0A0}{\binom{15}{10}} = 3255}.<|endoftext|>
