[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Find the maximum number of real roots to a polynomial of the form
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[x^n + x^{n - 1} + \dots + x + 1 = 0,\]where $n$ is a positive integer.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To determine the maximum number of real roots to the polynomial \( P(x) = x^n + x^{n-1} + \dots + x + 1 \), we first note that it is a polynomial with integer coefficients. The roots of \( P(x) \) are the \( n \)-th roots of a number that, when added to itself \( n-1 \) times, equals 0, which is exactly the condition for the number to be \(-1\). Therefore, the roots are:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ r_1, r_2, \ldots, r_n \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m where \( r_1, r_2, \ldots, r_n \) are the \( k \)-th roots of \(-1\). Since \(-1\) is a real number, all its \( k \)-th roots are real. Thus, \( P(x) \) has \( k \) real roots.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore, the maximum number of real roots to the polynomial \( x^n + x^{n-1} + \dots + x + 1 = 0 \) is \(\boxed{k}\).<|endoftext|>
