[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m My classroom has 11 rows of chairs, with 11 chairs in each row.  The chairs in each row are numbered from 1 to 11.  How many chairs have odd numbers?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find out how many chairs have odd numbers, we need to count the chairs that have odd numbers. We start by noting that the first chair is numbered 1, and the second is numbered 2, and so on until the last chair which is numbered 11. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Every odd-numbered chair will have unique numbers because changing anything from the 1 to the 11 numbers to another number constant will force the next chair to have the same set of numbers. For example, if we give a number 1 to chair number 1, and another number 3 to chair number 2, the chart above would look like this:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 1 - <empty>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2 - 3
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 3 - 5
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ...
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 11 - 11
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We can see that there's only one chair numbered 5 because any other number counts only once. So, the number of odd-numbered chairs can be found by counting those in the bottom right of the chart (down to chair number 11). 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Counting from 11 - 1, we get 10 chairs containing odd numbers per row. Lastly, adding these counts for both rows, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 10 chairs x 11 rows \[= 110\] 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore, our answer is 110 odd-numbered chairs.<|endoftext|>
