[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To compute the value of \[\cos(72^\circ)\], we can use the fact that \[e^{ix} = \cos(x) + i\sin(x)\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Expanding the cosine term, we get\[\cos(72^\circ) = \cos(72^\circ) +i\sin(72^\circ)\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, we can write out the rapid multiplication of complex numbers:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[[e^{i\theta}]\cdot \left[ 1 + i\sin(90 - \theta) \right] = e^{i\theta}(1 - i^2)\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Here, we need to determine the value of \(\sin(72^\circ)\) and \(\cos(72^\circ)\) properly. From trigonometry, we know that:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\sin(72^\circ) = \cos(18^\circ)\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Next, we need to find \(\cos(18^\circ)\). Using the Pythagorean identity, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\cos(18^\circ) = \sqrt{1 - \sin^2(18^\circ)}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We can then compute the value of \(\sin(72^\circ)\) and \(\cos(72^\circ)\) using the values aforementioned, thereby obtaining \(\cos(72^\circ)\) as the final result.<|endoftext|>
