[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m A sequence of positive real numbers $\{a_1, a_2, a_3, \dots\}$ has the property that for $i \ge 2,$ each $a_i$ is equal to the sum of all the previous terms.  If $a_{19} = 99,$ then what is $a_{20}$?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We have $a_1,2,\dots,20,$ and $a_2=99.$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m For $21,$ the sum of the first 20 terms is easy:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[a_1+\cdots+a_{20}=20a_{21}+99\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m This gets us $a_1=20(a_{21}-99).$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m For $22,$ we have $a_2+a_3+\cdots+a_{21}= 20(a_{22}-99)+99 = \begin{cases} 21(a_{23}-99) & a_{23}\ge 20 \\ 21(a_{21}-99)+20(a_{20}-99)& a_{23} < 20 \end{cases}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Note that the terms for $22$ would be increasing $\pmod 10$ and for $23$ would be decreasing $\pmod 10$ since each term is positive. Thus, we have a telescoping series.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m This pattern we see repeats with 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m a_1+a_2+ \cdots +a_{20} &= 99 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m a_2+a_3+ \cdots +a_{21}& = 21(a_2+ \cdots +a_{20})\\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m a_3+a_4+ \cdots +a_{22}& = 21(a_2+ \cdots +a_{21})\\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m &=\cardANGE{19}{i=2}a_i\\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m &= (20a_{23}-19 \cdot21),
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m and we can use this to find $a_{22}$ and $a_{23}$, and it goes into and out of range as we love to!  There are two cases:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Case 1: $a_{20}+99>21(a_{23}-99),$ so $a_{22}=a_{21}-2.$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m This limit is easily computed $(a_{21},a_{22}, \dots,a_{29})=(1101,99,12009,13208,15007,17146 \dots)$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Case 2: $a_{20}-10(a_{23}-99) \geq 0,$ so $a_{22}=29.$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m This is not a bad algebra! Again this limit is determined by $(a_{20}, a_{21},\dots,a_{29})=(1100, 667, 12009, 18525, 26298 \dots)$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The final answer is $\boxed{1994}.$_HERSHEYel<|endoftext|>
