[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The Ponde family's Powerjet pumps 420 gallons of water per hour. At this rate, how many gallons of water will it pump in 45 minutes?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the amount of water the Pumpel family can pump in 45 minutes, we first need to determine how many 60-minute intervals are in 45 minutes. We do this by dividing 45 minutes by 60 minutes:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ \frac{45 \text{ minutes}}{60 \text{ minutes/unit}} = \frac{1}{1.3333} \text{ or } \frac{1}{2.3333} \text{ or } \boxed{\frac{10}{26}} \text{ (rounded to two decimal places)} = \boxed{0.3815} \text{ gallons per minute}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, we use this rate to determine the total amount of water pumped in 45 minutes. We do this by multiplying the rate by the number of minutes:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 420 \text{ gallons/hour} \times 0.3815 \text{ gallons/minute} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m As we do these calculations:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 420 \text{ gallons/hour} \times 0.3815 \text{ gallons/minute} = 155.31 \text{ gallons/minute}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore: \boxed{155.31}<|endoftext|>
