[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m  Knoxville Indian Exchange is tracing back the heritage traces of a Native American Indian tribe from U.S. history as they reclaim their ancestral bands’ stories from the dusty walls of the state’s collection named The Mnogo-Adaloe. You will be taking part in the ”Nestigaminik Valley Liberation Procession ” for the first time!
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m What is a short summary of the above article? 1. The NPR report "Nestigaminik Valley Liberation Procession" was conducted as part of the "Closing Ceremony" at the KVOI affiliated Southeast Oklahoma Heritage Piedmont Chapter 2d House of Natural and Historic Records (HNorh). 2. During the parade, children, adults, and dignitaries gathered from all over the neighborhood to pay tribute to Native Americans who once made Kingston one of the most heavily commercialized communities in the country. 3. Annie Camur Pump Digger Oye, one of the descendants of Native Americans fighting to reclaim their ancestral bands’ stories from the dusty walls of the KVOI collection, took part in the procession with her grandson Gregory Ancestry Management. 4. Oye guided her grandson in a ceremony honoring Native Americans and their mining heritage by taking him through a history gallery that showed the impact of mining chemicals on native communities. 5. The purpose of the ceremony was to let the children and adults appreciate what lives paved in the Rough and Ready Mining Corporation's settlements at Kovo on which Kingston is located. 6. Gregory Ancestry Management learned the traditions of the Crow Indians, who first arrived in the Orrell Place area in the rough and ready mines in 1876 — a year before MGM established a boundary mine. The result was a history-gallery display that highlighted how coal mining has an impact on local communities, from contaminants to displacement. 7. At other sites throughout the local area not mentioned, the stained glass art (from the San Angelo Crusade House and Ranch Collection), and the jewelry and paintings handed down to the current owners all left visitors in awe.
