[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the greatest whole number that must be a divisor of the product of any three consecutive positive integers, we can start by considering the product of the first three positive integers and the last three positive integers.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The product of the first three positive integers (starting from 1) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 1 \times 2 \times 3 = 6 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The product of the last three positive integers (starting from 9) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 9 \times 10 \times 11 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 6 and 10 and 11. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 1. Find the GCD of 6 and 10. The prime factorization of 6 is \(2 \times 3\), and the prime factorization of 10 is \(2 \times 5\). The only common factor is 1, so \(\gcd(6, 10) = 1\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2. Now, find the GCD of 1 and 11. The prime factorization of 1 is \(1\), and the prime factorization of 11 is \(11\). The common factor is just 1, so \(\gcd(1, 11) = 1\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 3. The greatest common divisor of 6 and 10, and 11 is 1, but it is the greatest single divisor. The next divisor we should check is 11 since it is the largest single divisor of 6 and 11.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Consider divisibility of 11 by the factors of 10 and 9 (which are {2, 5}).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 1. Prime factorization of 10:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 10 = 2 \times 5 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2. Prime factorization of 11:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 11 \text{ is a prime number.} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Divisibility by 2 is \(2 \times 10 = 2 \times 5 = 10\). It is a multiple of 2.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Divisibility by 5 is \(5 \times 10 = 5 \times 5 = 25\), which is not a multiple of 2.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore, the greatest divisor of any three consecutive positive integers is 11.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
