[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Certainly! To determine how many 270-page books Robert can read in six hours, we need to break down the problem into smaller steps and solve it systematically.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 1. **Determine the rate of reading**: We know that Robert reads 90 pages per hour. Over the course of six hours, he will have read:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    6 \text{ hours} \times 90 \text{ pages/hour} = 540 \text{ pages}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2. **Calculate the total number of books he can read**: Since each book is 270 pages, we can find the number of books by dividing the total number of pages by the number of pages per book:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    \frac{540 \text{ pages}}{270 \text{ pages/book}} = 2
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    This calculation shows that Robert can read exactly two full 270-page books in six hours.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \boxed{2}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
