[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m First, note that \((\sec x + 1)\) and \(\sec x - 1\) are denominator, and are on numerator, i.e., \((\sec x + 1)\) is denominator for \(\frac{\tan x}{\sec x + 1} - \frac{\sec x - 1}{\tan x}\), and \(\sec x - 1\) is denominator for \(\frac{\tan x}{\sec x + 1} + \frac{\sec x - 1}{\tan x}\). According to the law of sines, we shorten to \(\frac{\tan x}{\frac{1}{\sin x} \cos x} - \frac{\frac{1}{\sin x} \cos x}{\frac{1}{\cos x}}\), which gives \(\left(\frac{\sin x}{\cos x}\right)^2 - (1 + \frac{1}{\sin x} \cos x)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Note that $\cos x = \sin \frac{\pi}{2}-\sin x$. Therefore, $\left(\frac{\sin x}{\cos x}\right)^2=1$, and $\cos x = \sin \frac{\pi}{2}-\sin x = \cos \frac{\pi}{2}+\sin x = \sin x $. So, the value is $1 - (1 + \sin x) = \sin x + \sin x - 1 = \sin x - 1$.
