[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the terminating decimal expansion of $\frac{11}{125}$, we need to calculate the dividend and divide it by the divisor, which is also known as the denominator. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The division is done as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\begin{array}{r|l}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 125 & 11\\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 25 & 1\\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \hline
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 0 & 11
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \end{array}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m This means the quotient is 0.08 and the remainder is 11`.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since the remainder is non-zero, we don't need to continue the division process. In other words, the decimal expansion terminates.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore, the terminating decimal expansion of $0.08$ repeating is 0.08.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
