[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m A church rings its bells every 15 minutes, the school rings its bells every 20 minutes and the day care center rings its bells every 25 minutes. If they all ring their bells at noon on the same day, at what time will they next all ring their bells together?  (Answer in the form AB:CD without am or pm, such as 08:00)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find when the church, school, and day care center will all ring their bells together again, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of their ringing intervals.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Step 1: Find the LCM of 15 (hens) and 20 (eighths) because the only time they differ is 15 (15 candles) and 20 (whole hours).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Step 2: Calculate LCM of 15 and 20.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - The prime factors of 15 are 3 and 5
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - The prime factors of 20 are 2 and 5
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - The LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor appearing in the list, which is 15 * 10 = 150.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - So, they will all ring their bells together 150 minutes later.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Step 3: Convert 150 minutes to hours.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - 150 minutes = 2 hours (since 60 minutes are one hour).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Step 4: Calculate the new time when the bells will ring together.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - If they ring at noon, add 2 hours to 12:00 noon.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - 12:00 noon + 2 hours = 7:00 PM.
