[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Alan saved 500 dollars in a bank account that compounds 3 percent annually.  Assuming there are no other transactions, after 10 years, how much is in Alan's bank account?  (Give your answer to the nearest dollar.)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Given that the bank account compounds annually and we want to know the amount after 10 years with a 3% annual interest rate, we can solve this using the compound interest formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Where:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m A = the future value of the investment / loan
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m P = the principal investment amount ($500)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m r = the annual interest rate (decimal) (3% = 0.03)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m n = the number of times that interest is compounded per year (1 for annually)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m t = the number of years the money is invested or borrowed (10 years)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Plugging in the values into the formula, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m A = $500(1 + 0.03/1)^(1*10)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m A = $500(1 + 0.03)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m A = $500 * 1.03
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m A = $515
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore, after 10 years, Alan would have $515 in his bank account.
