[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Sure. To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of 30, 90, and 75, we need to identify the prime factors of each number and determine the highest power of each prime factor that appears in all three numbers.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 1. Find the prime factorization of each number:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - \( 30 = 2 \times 3 \times 5 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - \( 90 = 3 \times 2 \times 3 \times 5 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - \( 75 = 3 \times 5^2 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2. Identify the common prime factors. In this case, the prime factors are 3 and 5.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 3. Determine the highest power of each common prime factor:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - The power of 3: \( 2 + 1 = 3 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - The power of 5: \( 1 + 2 = 3 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 4. The greatest common factor is the product of these highest powers:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    GCF = 3 \times 5^3 = 3 \times 125 = 375
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So, the greatest common factor of 30, 90, and 75 is \boxed{375}.<|endoftext|>
