[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m In this case, we have the angle $30$ degrees, which is in the first quadrant.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore, we can use the fact that $\sin (90°-x)=\cos x$ to rewrite $\cos 30^\circ$ as:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\cos 30^\circ =\sin (90°-30°)=\sin 60°$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since $60^{\circ}=90^{\circ}-30^{\circ}$, we can use the fact that $\sin (90°-x)=\cos x$ again to write $\sin 60^\circ$ as:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\sin 60^\circ =\cos 30^\circ$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Finally, we can plug the values into the equation and simplify:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\boxed{ \cfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2} }$
