[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m What is the quotient obtained when the polynomial $8x^6 - 9x^5 - 9x^4 + 6x^3 - 10x^2 - x + 1$ is divided by $(1 + x + 3x^3 + x^4)$. We can simplify the problem by considering the degree of the polynomial $f(x) = x^6 - 9x^5 + 9x^4 - 6x^3 + 10x^2 - x + 1$ and the divisor $g(x) = 1 + x + 3x^3 + x^4$. The degree of $f(x)$ is 6, and the degree of $g(x)$ is 4. Therefore, when $f(x)$ is divided by $g(x)$, the quotient and the remainder will be polynomials of degrees less than the degree of $g(x)$, i.e., $0$ and $g(x)$. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The general method to find the quotient of two polynomials is likely to involve expanding the modulus and a linear basis expansion of the divisor, but it is complex as noted in the response from AdvancedMath.
