[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Find the range of the function \[f(x) = \left\{ \begin{aligned} x^2 & \quad \text{ if } x \le 0 \\ x^3 - 5 & \quad \text{ if } x > 0. \end{aligned} \right.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the range of the function \( f(x) = \begin{cases} 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m x^2 & \text{if } x \le 0 \\ 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m x^3 - 5 & \text{if } x > 0 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \end{cases} \), we need to analyze both pieces of the function and determine the values they can take as \( x \) varies over all real numbers.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ### Step 1: Analyze \( x^2 \) for \( x \le 0 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m For \( x \le 0 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - When \( x = 0 \), \( f(0) = 0^2 = 0 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - As \( x \) increases from 0 towards \(-\infty\), \( f(x) = x^2 \) will take on all non-negative values less than or equal to 0.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since 0 is included in the range, and \( 0^2 = 0 \), the range for this part of the function is all non-negative real numbers up to and including 0. Therefore, we can write this as:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ x^2 \in [0, 0^+) = (0, 0^+) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ### Step 2: Analyze \( x^3 - 5 \) for \( x > 0 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m For \( x > 0 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - When \( x = 1 \), \( f(1) = 1^3 - 5 = 1 - 5 = -4 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - As \( x \) increases from 1 towards \(\infty\), \( f(x) = x^3 - 5 \) will take on all real values greater than -4.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since -4 is included in the range, we can write this as:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ x^3 - 5 \in [-4, \infty) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ### Step 3: Combine the ranges
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m From the analysis, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - For \( x \le 0 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m   \[ x^2 \in (0, 0^+) = (0, 0^+) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - For \( x > 0 \):
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m   \[ x^3 - 5 \in [-4, \infty) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Combining these, the complete range of the function \( f(x) \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ [0, 0^+) \cup (-4, \infty) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Thus, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \boxed{[0, 0^+) \cup (-4, \infty)}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
