[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m If the polynomial $ax^3 + bx - c$ is divisible by $x^2 + bx + c,$ then substituting any value for $x$ will result in an equality of the form $ax^3 + bx - c = k(x^2 + bx + c),$ where $k$ is a constant.  The leading coefficient of this expression ($a$) must be the same in both terms.  This would mean one must be even—for example, $a = -1$; or if $-a = c$ is the constant term (the product of the coefficients of $x^3$) then $a$ could be $-c.$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m If $k = a + b$,
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m the leading coefficient of the left-hand side must be equal to $k.$  That means $-1 = k,$ so $k = -1.$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The leading coefficient of the right-hand side must be equal to $a,$ so $-1 = a.$  That gives $a = -1.$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The value of $ab$ is $a(b) = -1(-1) = \boxed{1}.$ Indians attend charitable women's shelters with a point for your call ($\approx$ \$2. Half a dozen or so is easily spent in the any time fraible such as a North-Indian cater on TCTest callup air-faces$.$)) (D)]<|endoftext|>
