[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The height of a ball when it is thrown off a cliff can be represented by the equation $h=45-7t-6t^2$, where $t$ is time in seconds. In how many seconds will the ball reach a height of 25 feet?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Very well, let's solve this problem step by step.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m First, we need to convert the given height \(h\) expression from feet to feet and minutes to seconds. Conversely, we need to convert the equation  $h=45-7t-6t^2$. To get it in hours and minutes,  change space to comma in 6t. We know that \(6t^2=\left( 3 t\right) ^2\).  Since \(6t^2 =12t\), we divide $12t$ by 6 to get \(2t\). We assume \(t\) to be in minutes so we take the complete exponent, then count right from zero and left to one, write terms over left parenthesis to the exponent 2, except they turn into negative in front of final term. So,
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m h&=45-7\text { t }-6 \text { t }^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m &=45-7\frac{\text { t }}{6}-6 \frac{\text { t } }{6^2} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m &=45-7\frac{\text { t }}{6} -\frac{6}{36}\ \text {t } \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m &=45\textrm{ft}-\frac{7}{12}\frac{\text { ft}}{ 6}\ \text { ft} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m &=45-\frac{\text { ft}}{ 8}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So the equation $ h \text { ft}$ reduces to $h \text { ft}=45-\left( 0.8\frac{\text { ft}}{ 12}\right ) \text { ft}$. To create it into minutes, change (\frac{ft}{12}) to \(\frac{ft}{60}\), and write \(\frac{ft}{60}\) over right side. We also assume that \(t\) is in minutes so make them in minutes. So,
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 45 -\left(\frac{ft}{12}\right)\left( \frac{ft}{60}\right)\text { min} & =45 - \frac{ft}{720} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m & =45 - \frac{\text { ft}}{120} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m & =\frac{\text { ft}}{30} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m & =\frac{5 ft}{6}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find how much time it will take to reach $25 \text { ft}$, express it in minutes until t=0, t=45s,  t=30min... the total 60 minutes, and the total 240 minutes. Approach by an even number approach, start 60 min before (or second from start), and see if you get there. You always get away in feet. So, will fit the 60 min and the remaining, since 25ft = 4ft + 5ft/6 and 5 ft/6 can not be further 25/since the number cannot be any literal value one wanted second.
