[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the 125th digit after the decimal point of the fraction \(\frac{4}{7}\), we need to perform the necessary arithmetic operations to isolate the repeating decimal.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \frac{4}{7} = 0.\overline{571428}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m This means that when you divide 4 by 7, you get 0.571428571428\ldots\, (repeating). The repeating part starts after "571428".
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the 125th digit after the decimal point, we can perform an integer division and record the remainder at each step. The steps for the division are as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 1. Divide 4 by 7 to find the first repetition: 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 4 \div 7 = [0, 0, 6] \, (\overline{6})
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2. Bring down the next 0 in front of the repeating sequence: 
