[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m For real numbers $x > 1,$ find the minimum value of
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\frac{x + 8}{\sqrt{x - 1}}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the minimum value, we can take the derivatives of the function and set them to equal zero.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\begin{align*}f'(x) &= \frac{(x+8)'(x-1) - (x+8)(x-1)'}{(x-1)^{3/2}}\\ &= \frac{(1)(x-1) - (x+8)}{(x-1)^{3/2}}\\ &= \frac{x-1 - (x+8)}{(x-1)^{3/2}}\\ &= \frac{-9}{(x-1)^{3/2}}. \end{align*}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Setting $f'(x) = 0$, we get $f'(x) = \frac{-9}{(x-1)^{3/2}} = 0$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since $x > 1$, the solution to this equation is $x = 1 + 2\sqrt{3}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the minimum value, we can plug this value back into the original function:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\frac{(1+2\sqrt{3})+(8)}{\sqrt{(1+2\sqrt{3})-1}} = \frac{9+\sqrt{3}}{\sqrt{2\sqrt{3}}}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m However, since this value is greater than 1, we can check if there's another solution to the equation.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since the derivative is a cubic polynomial, it will have a minimum value when it's positive. So, the critical point we found is actually the minimum value of the given function.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ####
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m boxed{9 + $z$}<|endoftext|>
