[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m For real numbers $x > 1,$ find the minimum value of
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\frac{x + 8}{\sqrt{x - 1}}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{aligned} f(x) &= \frac{x + 8}{\sqrt{ x - 1}} \\ f' (x) &= \frac{ \sqrt{x - 1} \left(1 - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x - 1}}\right) - (x + 8)\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x - 1}}}{(x - 1)} \\ f' (x) &= \frac{1 - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x - 1}} - \frac{x + 8}{2\sqrt{x - 1}}}{(x - 1)} \\ f' (x) &= \frac{1 - \frac{x + 7}{2\sqrt{x - 1}}}{(x - 1)} \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, let's make a substitution to find the critical points of $f'(x)$. Lets let $x=2+t$, where $t$ is a constant. Then $x - 1 = 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{aligned} f'(t) &= \frac{1 - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{(2 + t) - 1}}}{(2 + t - 1)} \\ f'(t) &= \frac{1 - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{ t + 1}}}{1 + t} \\ f'(t) &= \frac{2(1 + t) \left(1 - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{ t + 1}}\right)}{(2 + t)(1 + t)} \\ f'(t) &= \frac{1 - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{t + 1}}}{2 + t} \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, we make the computation 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{aligned} f'(t) &= \frac{1 - \frac{1}{2\sqrt{t + 1}}}{2 + t} \\ f'(t) &= \frac{ 2 + t - 1}{(2 + t)(2\sqrt{t + 1} + 1)} \\ f'(t) &= \frac{t + 1}{(2 + t)(2\sqrt{t + 1} + 1)} \\ \end{aligned} and using the inequality $y + \frac{\pi}{6} < x < y +\pi$, with $y = \sqrt{t +1}$ and $x = t +2$, this implies to $f'(x) < 0$ for $t > -1$. This means $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing for $x > |-1| = 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So, there is a global maximum of the function, given by $f(1)$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{aligned} f(1) &= \frac{1 + 8}{\sqrt{1 - 1}} \\ f(1) &= \frac{1 + 8}{\sqrt{0}} \\ f(1) &= 9 \end{aligned}
