[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the sum of the distinct prime factors of 315, we first need to identify its prime factors. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 315 is divisible by the smallest prime number, 2. 315 ÷ 2 = 157. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 315 is not divisible by 2 again, so the next prime factor is 3. 315 ÷ 3 = 105. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 105 is also divisible by 3, as 105 ÷ 3 = 35. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 35 is also divisible by 3, as 35 ÷ 3 = 11.5, so we can round it down to 11. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 11 is also divisible by 3, as 11 ÷ 3 = 3.67, so we can round it down to 3. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 11 and 3 are both prime numbers, so we finally have 3 prime factors. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The distinct prime factors of 315 are 3, 5, and 11. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the sum of these distinct prime factors, we simply add them together. 3 + 5 + 11 = 0 + 5 + 11 = 0 + 16. 5 + 11 + 0 = 0 + 16. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore, the sum of the distinct prime factors of 315 is 0 + 16 = 16. 
