[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Circle $A$ has a diameter of 12 inches and circle $B$ has a diameter of 22 inches. What is the ratio of the area of circle $A$ to the area of circle $B$? Express your answer as a common fraction.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the ratio of the area of circle $A$ to the area of circle $B$, we first need to understand the formula for the area of a circle.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The area of a circle is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \text{Area} = \pi \times r^2
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m where $r$ is the radius of the circle.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m In both circle $A$ and circle $B$, the radius is the diameter divided by 2. So:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \text{Radius of circle } A = \frac{12}{2} = 6 \text{ inches}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \text{Radius of circle } B = \frac{22}{2} = 11 \text{ inches}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, let's calculate the area of each circle:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \text{Area of circle } A = \pi \times 6^2 = 36\pi \text{ square inches}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \text{Area of circle } B = \pi \times 11^2 = 121\pi \text{ square inches}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Finally, to find the ratio of the area of circle $A$ to the area of circle $B$, we divide the area of circle $A$ by the area of circle $B$:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \text{Ratio of the areas} = \frac{\text{Area of circle } A}{\text{Area of circle } B} = \frac{36\pi}{121\pi}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To simplify this fraction, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by $\pi$:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \frac{36\pi}{121\pi} = \frac{36}{121}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So, the ratio of the area of circle $A$ to the area of circle $B$ is $\frac{36}{121}$.<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \frac{36}{121}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
