[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Let's break this down step-by-step using Python to calculate the sum of the series \(1 + 2 + 3 + \cdots + 98 + 99 + 100\). We can use an iterative approach here to avoid checking indices which can be very slow for large numbers like 100.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The sum \(S\) of the series can be represented as:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ S = \sum_{i=1}^{99} i \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m This series can be calculated using the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ \text{Sum} = \frac{n(n+1)}{2} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m where \(n\) is the number of terms.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ n = 99 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ S = \frac{99 \times (99 + 1)}{2} = \frac{99 \times 100}{2} = 9900 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Below is the Python code to compute this sum.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ```python
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m # Calculate the sum of the series 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + 98 + 99 + 100
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m S = sum(range(1, 100))
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m print(S)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ```
