[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Find the solution to $x|x| = 2x+1$ which has the smallest value.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Let $f(x) = \begin{cases} 2x+1, & x \geq 0 \\ -x^2+1, & x < 0 \end{cases}$, then $f^{-1}(x) = \begin{cases} - \sqrt{\dfrac{x}{2}+1}, & x > 0 \\ \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x+4}}, & x \leq 0 \end{cases}$, i.e., $x = \sqrt{\dfrac{f^{-1}(x)}{2}+1}$, and $x=-\sqrt{\dfrac{-(f^{-1}(x))}{2}-1}$ are the minimum values.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x+4}} = \sqrt{x+4} \cdot \dfrac{2}{\sqrt{x+4}} \geqslant \dfrac{2}{\sqrt{x+4}} = f^{-1}(x)$, the minimum value is $-\sqrt{\dfrac{-(f^{-1}(x))}{2}-1}$, which can be simplified as $-\sqrt{\dfrac{-\dfrac{f^{-1}(x)}{2}-1}{2}-1}$, yielding $\boxed{-\dfrac{5}{4}}$.
