[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m In a class of $30$ students, exactly $7$ have been to Mexico and exactly $11$ have been to England. Of these $30$ students, $4$ have been to both Mexico and England. How many students in this class have not been to Mexico or England?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Let $m$ be the number of students who have been to Mexico, $n$ be the number of students who have been to England, and $p$ be the number of students who have been to both. Since there are $30$ students in total, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[m + n - p = 30.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We also know that:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[m - n = 7,\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[n - p = 11,\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[n + m - p - 4 = 4.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Substituting the values from the first few equations into the last equation gives:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\left(7\right) + \left(11\right) + \left(p - 7\right) - 4 = 30.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Simplifying yields:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[8 + p - 11 = 30,\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[p = 20.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Substituting the value of $p$ into the equation $n - p = 11$ gives:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[n - 20 = 11,\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[n = 31.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So the number of students who have not been to Mexico or England is $\boxed{31}$.我不是计算机程序算法，但可以提供数学解决方案作为答案。lers
