[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We are asked to find the remainder when $x^4 + 1$ is divided by $x^2 - 3x + 5$. First, notice that the divisor has a degree of 2 (both x^2 and $-x + 5$), so we can apparently factor it. So first, I will find the roots of the divisor (if it's polynomial), which would be tied 3, -5. And indeed; factoring the divisor gives us $(x-3)(x-5)$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, the remainder would not include the factors of the divisor, so I try to try one itself. I start with $x^2$, which has no roots, so I try one of the clues it has.
