[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To convert the given spherical coordinates \((\rho, \theta, \phi) = \left( 3, \frac{5 \pi}{12}, 0 \right)\) to rectangular coordinates \((x, y, z)\), we can use the following formulas:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m x &= \rho \sin \phi \cos \theta, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m y &= \rho \sin \phi \sin \theta, \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m z &= \rho \cos \phi.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Given:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \left( \rho, \theta, \phi \right) = \left( 3, \frac{5 \pi}{12}, 0 \right)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Using \(\phi = 0\), the formula simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m x = \rho \sin \phi \cos \theta = 3 \sin 0 \cdot \cos \frac{5\pi}{12} = 0
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m y = \rho \sin \phi \sin \theta = 3 \sin 0 \cdot \sin \frac{5\pi}{12} = 0
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m z = \rho \cos \phi = 3 \cos 0 = 3
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Thus, the rectangular coordinates \( (x, y, z) \) are:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \boxed{(0, 0, 3)}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
