[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m If $n$ is a multiple of three, what is the remainder when $(n + 4) + (n + 6) + (n + 8)$ is divided by $9$?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To solve this problem, let's first simplify the expression on the left side of the equation.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The expression simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ (n+4) + (n+6) + (n+8) = 3n + 18 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We are asked to find the remainder when \(3n + 18\) is divided by 9. To do this, we can perform the division and see what remainder we get.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m First, let's compute the remainder of \(3n\) when divided by 9, since \(18\) divided by \(9\) gives a quotient of \(2\) and a remainder of \(0\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Then, we can add the remaining \(18\) to \(3n\) and find the remainder again when divided by \(9\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 3n \text{ can be written as } 9k + r_1 \quad (r_1 \text{ is the remainder}) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m where \(9k\) is a multiple of 9.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So,
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 3n + 18 \text{ on the right side of the equation} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ (3n \text{ remaining when dividing by 9} + 18) \text{ will be added to this new remainder} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since \(r_1\) is the quotient of 3 times \(n\) divided by 9, the remainder \(r_1\) when 3 is divided by 9 will be what goes into \(3n\). The remainder when \(3n\) is divided by 9 is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 3r_1 \text{ (if } r_1 \text{ is the preferred remainder)} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m but the smallest result is typically derived from the division \(r_1 \text{ times \(n\)}\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Given \(r_1 = 3\):
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 3(3n) = 9n \implies 9n \text{ where that equals } 9(3) = 27 \text{ when divided by 9 geometry!}\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ (3n + 18) \text{ becomes } (9n + 18) \mod 9\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore, the remainder when \(3n + 18\) is divided by 9 is 18.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ![Perform the correct code to illustrate](https://i.imgur.com/fjRglRQ.png)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ####
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The answer is 1<|endoftext|>
