[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Let $m$ be the smallest positive, three-digit integer congruent to 5 (mod 11). Then $m = 100 + 3k$ for some integer $k$, and $m$ is congruent to 16 (mod 11). That is $5 + 11(k - 1) \equiv 5 \pmod{11}$, $k \equiv 0$. So $m = 100 + 6, = 66$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m  sets $n$ be the three-digit integer congruent to 5 (mod 11), so $n = 100 + 11(t-1) = 100 + 11t \equiv 5 \pmod{11}$, so $t = 1 + 11(k)$, and $m = 66 + 11(t-1) = 66 + 11( 11k + 1) = 77 + 77k \equiv 16 \pmod{11} $
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m And we can now find \ [m-n=$77+77k-66-16=\boxed{{} 16k+8}}]<|endoftext|>
