[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ### Step 6: Check the feedback loop
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ q(t) = P x(p) y(p) + I y(t) + D x(t) u(t) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since \(q(t)\) should satisfy both equations, we can find the values of \(P\), \(I\), and \(D\) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 2q(t) = 2x(t) x(p) y(t) + y(t) u(t) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ D q(t) = 2x(t) x(q) y(t) + y(t) u(t) \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ### Step 7: Verify the system
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Given the connectivity and the constraints, we can determine the coefficients by solving the system of linear equations formed by combining the given equations.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ### Final Analytical Approach
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Given the constraints and the devices provided, we can analyze the system analytically and find that using the proportional feedback with a rate derivative term will stabilize the system. The ID conversion will help us get about any desired feedback gain without resorting to a PID.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ### Conclusion
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m After analyzing the system and connecting the given equations, we can conclude that a constant proportional feedback with a rate derivative term will ensure that:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 2x^2 + 1y + z = 0 \] and
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 2x^2 + y + 3z = 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
