[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the value of \(x\), we need to consider the Triangle Sum Theorem. The Triangle Sum Theorem states that the sum of the internal angles of any triangle is 180 degrees.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Given triangle \(ABC\) with appropriate side lengths, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - \( BC = 4 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - \( AC = 1 \)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - \( AB = 8 \) (since \( x = 4\) is the external angle and the triangle is an acute triangle)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Applying the Triangle Sum Theorem:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ \angle A + \angle B + \angle C = 180^\circ \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since \( ∠ A = 150^\circ \) and \( \angle B = 4^\circ \), we can solve for \( \angle C \):
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 150^\circ + 4^\circ + \angle C = 180^\circ \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ \angle C = 180^\circ - 150^\circ - 4^\circ \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ \angle C = 25^\circ \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Thus, \( x = 25^\circ \).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Putting it all together in Python:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \boxed{25^\circ}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]<|endoftext|>
