[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Find the matrix $\mathbf{P}$ such that for any vector $\mathbf{v},$ $\mathbf{P} \mathbf{v}$ is the projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto the $yz$-plane.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the projection matrix $\mathbf{P}$, we need to find a vector in the $yz$-plane and solve for the projection of the given vector $\mathbf{v}$ onto the $yz$-plane. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Let's assume the given vector is $\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto the $yz$-plane is the vector $\mathbf{o}$, so the projection matrix $\mathbf{P}$ is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\mathbf{P} = \frac{\mathbf{v} \mathbf{v}^T}{\mathbf{v}^T \mathbf{v}}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m First, let's calculate $\mathbf{v} \mathbf{v}^T$:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\mathbf{v} \mathbf{v}^T = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x & y & z \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} x^2 & xy & xz \\ xy & y^2 & yz \\ xz & yz & z^2 \end{bmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Next, let's calculate $\mathbf{v}^T \mathbf{v}$:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\mathbf{v}^T \mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} x & y & z \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} x^2 & xy & xz \\ xy & y^2 & yz \\ xz & yz & z^2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} x^3 & x^2y & x^2z \\ x^2y & y^3 & y^2z \\ x^2z & y^2z & z^3 \end{bmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, let's calculate $\mathbf{P}$:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\mathbf{P} = \frac{1}{\mathbf{v}^T \mathbf{v}} \mathbf{v} \mathbf{v}^T = \frac{1}{\begin{bmatrix} x^3 & x^2y & x^2z \\ x^2y & y^3 & y^2z \\ x^2z & y^2z & z^3 \end{bmatrix}} \begin{bmatrix} x^2 & xy & xz \\ xy & y^2 & yz \\ xz & yz & z^2 \end{bmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Simplifying the expression, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\mathbf{P} = \frac{1}{x^3} \begin{bmatrix} x^6 & x^5y & x^4y + xz \\ x^5y & x^4y & x^4z + x^2z \\ x^5y & x^4z + x^2z & x^4z + xz + y^6 + y^2z + z^6 \end{bmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\mathbf{P} = \frac{1}{x^3 \cdot y^3 \cdot z^3} \begin{vmatrix} x^6 & x^5y & x^4y + xz \\ x^5y & x^4y & x^4z + x^2z \\ x^5y & x^4z + x^2z & x^4z + xz + y^6 + y^2z + z^6 \end{vmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the projection matrix $\mathbf{P}$, we need to replace $\frac{x^4z + xz + y^6 + y^2z + z^6}{x^9}$ with $\frac{z}{y}$ in the expression above. This gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\mathbf{P} = \frac{1}{x^3} \begin{bmatrix} x^6 & x^5y & x^4y + xz \\ x^5y & x^4y & x^4z + x^2z \\ x^5y & x^4z + x^2z & x^4z + xz + y^6 + y^2z + z^6 \end{bmatrix} \cdot \frac{y^3 \cdot z^3}{1} = \begin{bmatrix} y^6 & y^5y & y^4y + y^3z \\ y^5y & y^4y & y^4z + yz \\ y^5y & y^4z + yz & y^4z + yz + y^6 + y^2z + z^6 \end{bmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So, the projection matrix $\mathbf{P}$ for any vector $\mathbf{v} = \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}$ that we're looking for is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\boxed{\begin{bmatrix} y^6 & y^5y & y^4y + y^3z \\ y^5y & y^4y & y^4z + yz \\ y^5y & y^4z + yz & y^4z + yz + y^6 + y^2z + z^6 \end{bmatrix}}$<|endoftext|>
