[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m A dilation centered at the origin takes $\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$ to $\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ -21 \end{pmatrix}.$  Which vector does the dilation take $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}$ to?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since you took the left side vector $\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$ to the center, which is the origin (0, 0), you need to do the same modification on the right side vector, so after the dilation:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} \rightarrow \begin{pmatrix} 4/5 \\ 7/5 \end{pmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} \rightarrow \begin{pmatrix} \frac{-2\cdot 5-12\cdot -2}5 \\ \frac{-2\cdot 5+\12\cdot 5}{5} \end{pmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} \rightarrow \begin{pmatrix} -2 \cdot \frac{-2}{5}-12 \cdot \frac{-2}{5} \\ 5 \cdot \frac{-2}{5}+\12\cdot \frac{-2}{5} \end{pmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} \rightarrow \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -11 \end{pmatrix}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m There, answer let us figure out how mutating a vector (X,Y) to the center of dilation (c,d), where d is defined by this formula: 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $d=\frac{X-c}{X^2+c^2},c=\frac{Y-d}{Y^2+d^2}$.
