[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The U.S. produces about 5.5 million tons of apples each year. Of the total, $20\%$ is mixed with other products, with $50\%$ of the remainder used for apple juice and the other $50\%$ sold fresh. How many million tons of apples are used for apple juice? Express your answer as a decimal to the nearest tenth.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Let our answer be \( 20 \bar{5}\% \). Then when converted to a decimal as %, that converts to 0.2. Since 20 \bar{5}\% represents \( 20\% \) of the total, then if the total is \( T \) \( \bar{grams} \), then in this equation \( 0.\bar{5}\times0.2 T \) represents two \( \bar{-grams} \). That is, \( 20 \bar{5}\% = 20 \bar{-grams} \). In words, 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[ 20 \times \frac{1}{100} \times 20 \bar{-grams} = 20 \bar{-grams} \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Thus, \( 207 \bar{grams} \) is equivalent to \( 2070 \bar{grams} \), finally, any other conversion to \( T \) grams. Using our classification, the equation now becomes \( T \times \frac{1}{100} \times 20 \bar{grams} \), simplifying to \( 207 \bar{grams} \times \frac{T}{100} = 207 \bar{grams} T \% \).  
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, \(\frac{207}{100}\) \( \% \) is \( 207\bar{grams} \) per.  Hence, every \( 200 \bar{grams} \) we consume is its equivalent to \( 207 \bar{grams} \) per. Each multiple of 200 \( g \) bottles they consume are equivalent to \( 200 \times 207 = 40940 \bar{grams} \).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Thus, every \( 200 \bar{grams} \) they consume is equivalent to 200 \( \bar{grams} \) in apples. So, **\( 407 \bar{grams} \) of apples are consumed in 200 \bar{grams} of bottles.**
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m **boxed{407}$
