[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The problem given in the diagram is an equation for an angle named $x$. To solve this, we need to translate the angles from radians into degrees and then solve for $x$. The equation is $2x + 3\pi /180 = 3x.$ This can be simplified using the similar angles formula for pi/180 degrees for $2x$ and $3x$. Since the similar angles formula states that $\tan(\pi/180) = \tan(2x - 3x)$, the distance between the $x$-axis and the terminal side of $x^\circ$ i.e. "angle" $2x$ is the same as "angle" $3x.$ Hence, any single value for $x$ will lead to an ambiguous situation for the $2x$ angle around the $x$-axis because the negative binomially possible values lie off the $ \pm \pi/180$ circle. For which is $2x$ the "odd" or "completed" angle closest to $x$ unless a specific extension which is not well-defined due to darkish angles around the Cartesian plane. If we were to truncate the circle and consider $0$ and $\pi/180$ being on **ends** of the circle, then one would usually round $2x$ to the highest point of the circle or the smallest angle in the circle which is $\pi/180$ since angle's radian measures are approximate as well.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The equidistant polar metric takes into account two assumptions:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m * $x$ cannot be greater than $\pi/180$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ```
