[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m First, let's understand the function \( f(x) \). 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The expression inside the cosine function is \( \cos(x - 51.3^0 - b) \). As \( x \) approaches the value where \(\cos\) achieves its maximum value (\( \cos(\pi) \)), the function \( f(x) \) will approach the value of \(\cos(\pi) = -1\). This is because \(\cos(\pi + b) = -\cos(b)\), and for small values of \( b \), \(\cos(b) \approx -1\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Next, let's consider the given choices. We need to determine which one is outside the function's range.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Here are the choices:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 1. 1
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2. -3
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 3. -6
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 4. 9
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since \( \cos(\pi) = -1 \), the expression \( \cos(x - 51.3^0 - x) \) will approximate \( \cos(\pi) \) as \( x \) varies over intervals symmetric around \(-51.3\):
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - As \( x \to -51.3^+ \), \(\cos(x - 51.3) \approx \cos(\pi + b)\) approaches \(-1\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - As \( x \to -51.3^-\), \(\cos(x - 51.3) \approx \cos(\pi - b)\) approaches \(1\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The only value of \( x \) where \(\cos(x - 51.3) \) generally approaches \(-1\) is on the negative side of -51.3, where \(\cos\) is closest to \(-1\) in magnitude. Thus, \( \cos(x - 51.3^0 - b) \) will also generally approach \(-1\) for values of \( x \) close to -51.3.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Let's summarize the conditions:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - For \( x \) big, \( x > -2 \), \( f(x) = 1 \) because \(\cos(x - 51.3^0 - b) \approx (-1)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - For \( x < -2 \), \( f(x) = -1 \) because \(\cos(x - 51.3^0 - b) \approx (-1)\).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m This means that the range of \( f(x) \) is bounded between \( -2 \) and \( 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m On the boundary values:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - For small \( x \), \( f(x) \) will be similar to \( -2 \) or \( 1 \) due to the cosine terms' behavior as stated above.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Thus, among the choices provided, no integer is outside the range of \( f(x) \):
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - 1 (the first choice)
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m - -3 (not in the range)
