[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Your friend has an egg collection comprising at least $200$ eggs. He wants to store them in dozen-egg containers. After filling as many containers as possible, the last container had $1$ egg left over. He then decided to store his eggs in customized baker-dozen-egg containers, where each container can hold $13$ eggs. It turns out that, after filling as many of these containers as possible, he still has $1$ egg left over. What is the minimum number of eggs that your friend could have?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Welcome to the world of interactive challenges! Let's dive right in.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To solve this problem, we need to apply the concept of linear Diophantine equations. We can express the egg collection collection using the variables \( x_1 \) (eggs) and \( x_2 \) (bakers).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 1. **Both the basic egg containers and baker-dozen egg containers can hold integer number of eggs.**
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 2. **If \( x_2^2 + x_1 \) is divisible by 13**:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - Then the standard baker-dozen egg containers can accommodate the egg collection without any leftover (modulo 13).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 3. **Every baker-dozen egg container can hold 13 eggs**.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 4. **We are given extra eggs left after all containers are filled**:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m    - After filling all containers, it leaves 1 leftover egg.
