[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\cos 90^\circ$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m DataContract, we have $\cos 90^\circ = 0$. Physically, the cosine of $90^\circ$ is the extension of the unit circle where it's the $y$-coordinate of the point $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ X \end{pmatrix}$. To understand why we are considering the point $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ X \end{pmatrix}$, let's recall two crucial definitions for $\cos$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\text{If a point makes an angle $\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis and its coordinates are $(\cos \theta, \sin \theta)$, then:}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \theta: \cos \theta = \text{the $x$-coordinate} \\ \sin \theta = \text{the $y$-coordinate}$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find $\cos 90^\circ$:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m If consider that one of the angles corresponding to the point is $90^\circ$, out of all possible angles corresponding to the same point, $90^\circ$ makes the shortest angle, that's why $\cos 90^\circ = 0$.
