[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m In a particular list of three-digit perfect squares, the first perfect square can be turned into each of the others by rearranging its digits. What is the largest number of distinct perfect squares that could be in the list?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To solve this problem, we should first notice that the original three-digit perfect squares have a form like this: \( (10a + b)(100a + 10b + c) \). According to the condition provided, we must rearrange the digits in such a way that the original square can become a new square. Let's break down the rearrangement steps. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Given elements will be reordered as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m S_1 &= \text{(first two digits)}m \times \text{(first three digits)}l + \text{(first digit)}c \times \text{(second three digits)} \cdot \text{(even third digit)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m S_2 &= \text{(first digit)}a \times \text{(second two digits)} + \text{(first digit)} \times (10b + c) \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m S_3 &= \text{(first two digits)}m \times \text{(first three digits)}l + \text{(first digit)}c \times \text{(third digit)}.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m i.e. we can write:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m S_1 &= \dfrac{\text{(second digit)} \cdot (A*10 + B \times 10 + C)}{\text{(first digit)} \cdot (D^2)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m S_2 &= \dfrac{\text{(third digit)} \times (E^2)}{\text{(second digit)} \cdot (AB + CD - E)} \\
