[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Find the matrix $\mathbf{M}$ that swaps the columns of a matrix.  In other words,
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\mathbf{M} \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} b & a \\ d & c \end{pmatrix}.\]If no such matrix $\mathbf{M}$ exists, then enter the zero matrix.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Ok, first, let's rewrite the condition as:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\mathbf{M} \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} a & a \\ d & c \end{pmatrix}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Then, we can't just check whether $\mathbf{M}$ is the identity matrix,  
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m because if it is, there's no $\mathbf{M}$ that works.  
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, let's find a matrix $\mathbf{m}$ that satisfies the equation.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since $\mathbf{M}$ changes the x-coordinate, $a$ and $a$ should be changing this way:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$\begin{pmatrix} \text{m}_1(\text{a}, \text{a}) & \text{m}_2(\text{a}, \text{a}) \\ \text{m}_3(\text{a}, \text{a}) & \text{m}_4(\text{a}, \text{a}) \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \text{p}_1(a, \text{a}) \\ \text{p}_2(a, \text{a}) \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \text{p}_1(a, \text{a}) & \text{p}_2(a, \text{a}) \\ \text{p}_3(a, \text{a}) & \text{p}_4(a, \text{a}) \end{pmatrix}$$
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m which means that $\text{p}_1(a, \text{a}) = \text{p}_3(a, \text{a}) = \text{m}_4(\text{a}, \text{a})$ and $\text{p}_2(a, \text{a}) = \text{p}_4(\text{a}, \text{a}) = \text{m}_2(\text{a}, \text{a})$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Similarly, $\text{m}_3 = \text{p}_2$ and $\text{m}_4 = \text{p}_3$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Now, let's find a $2 \times 2$ matrix $\mathbf{m}$ that shows this information.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\mathbf{m} = \begin{pmatrix} m_{11} & m_{12} \\ m_{21} & m_{22} \end{pmatrix}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m m_{11} & = \text{m}_4(\text{a}, \text{a}) = \text{p}_3(a, \text{a}) = m_{43}(\text{a}, \text{a})\\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m m_{12} & = \text{m}_2(\text{a}, \text{a}) = m_{24}(\text{a}, \text{a}) = m_{34}(\text{a}, \text{a})
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m These values will tell us that the matrix $\mathbf{M}$ that swaps the columns is:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}}.\]<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
