[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m In the unit circle, given the unit radius, the wide sides of the quadrilateral span this radius at a distance of \sqrt{2} as the distance between the circle's endpoints \[(r,\theta) \rightarrow (r\cos(\theta), r\sin(\theta))\]. In this coordinate system, in the \(xy-plane\), putting the M\text{%} based origin at the \((0,0)\) point and the \((r,\theta)\) coordinates of the largest point as \((x,\theta)\), where \(\theta\) ranges from \(0\) to \(2\pi\):
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{matrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \ i \ 2\pi & \frac{3\pi}{2} & \pi\\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \ topusta parecelyo   &   0   &   \frac{\pi}{2}   \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \ rhs Hungary  &    \pi  &    \pi 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \end{matrix}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m As the largest point remains fixed in such a manner, \( y = \theta = \frac{\pi}{2}\), therefore, \((x,\theta) = (x, \frac{\pi}{2})\):
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m x = \cos(\frac{\pi}{2}) = 0,
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m z = \sin(\frac{\pi}{2}) = 1.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So, the area of this rectangle as a fraction is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m A = x\theta = 0 \times \frac{\pi}{2} = 0.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \]
