[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Sam the Sham is an unfair shoe salesman. He has a pair of shoes that was priced at $\$60$ last week. To entice customers, he wants to put a "$25\%$ off'' tag on the shoes but he still wants the customer to pay $\$60$ for the shoes. He raises the price of the shoes so that after the $25\%$ discount the shoes will cost $\$60$. In dollars, what must the new price of the shoes be before the discount?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To find the new price of the shoes after a 25% discount, we first need to calculate the amount of the discount.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 25% of $60 is $\frac{25}{100} \cdot 60 = \$15$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m This means that Sam has to give the customer $\$60 - \$15 = \$45$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since his customer had to pay $\$60', we can set $\$45$ as the discounted price and solve for the original price.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \text{discount price} &= \text{original price} - \text{discount amount} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \$45 &= \$60 - \$15 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Therefore, the new price of the shoes before the 25% discount is $\$45$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m ####
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $\boxed{45}$<|endoftext|>
