[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Fiona is people-watching again. She spies a group of ten high schoolers and starts playing a game by herself, in which she looks at a pair of people from the group of ten and tries to guess whether they like or dislike each other. How many pairs of friends can she observe before she runs out of pairs to evaluate?
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m To determine the number of pairs Fiona can observe before reaching exhaustion, we need to understand the rules of the game. Each person is paired with another person in the group. Therefore, if there are 10 people in total, Fiona can pair each of the 5 remaining people with one of these 10 people. 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The number of unique pairs she can form is given by the combination formula for choosing 2 people out of 10, which is "10 choose 2" or "site multiply notation. Therefore, the required number of pairs is \( 10 \times 9 \div 2 = 45 \).
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m So, Fiona can observe pairs of friends from the group in \boxed{45} before she runs out of pairs to evaluate. deportationfulstomek
