[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Let $z_1$, $z_2$, $z_3$, $\dots$, $z_{12}$ be the 12 zeroes of the polynomial $z^{12} - 2^{36}$. For each $j$, let $w_j$ be one of $z_j$ or $iz_j$.  Find the maximum possible value of the real part of
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m \[\sum_{j = 1}^{12} w_j.\]
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m AoL system
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Ao~
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We are given 12 nonzero complex numbers $4^3 z_k$, $k = 1, \dots, 12$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We need to maximize the real part of $\sum_{k = 1}^{12} z_k$ given that $z_k = w_k i$ for any $w_k \in \mathbb{R}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The quadratic equation that this set of 12 complex numbers represents is $(4^3 + z^6) w^2 = 0$, I claim that the maximum real part of $\sum_{k = 1}^{12} z_k$ is $\frac{1}{4^3 \cdot 288}$.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Since we already have the real part of $\sum_{k = 1}^{12} z_k$ assigned to it, we can assign 15 more complex numbers so that the real part of $\sum_{k = 1}^{12} z_k + w_1 + \dots + w_{15}$ is maximized.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m We resolve the quadratic equation in each of the new 15 complex numbers as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m $$(4^3 + z^{611} w^2) (4^3 - z^{611} w^2) = 0 \implies (4^3)^2 - z^{12 \cdot 611} w^2 + \dots + z^{611} (4^3 - z^{611} w^2) = 0.$$ 
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Thus, the sum of the four complex numbers on the left-hand side is given by \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m 176^3(4^3)^2 - \frac{2 \cdot 17!}{177} \cdot 15^3z^6 +\dots &= (4^3)^6 = 42952945832 \text{ or  }0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m &= (4^3)^6/1768 = 445737176 / 288 = \frac{1}{4^3 \cdot 288} \\
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m &= \frac{84001}{4194304} = \boxed{\frac{1}{4^3 \cdot 288}}.
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m The answer is: \frac{1}{43888}<|endoftext|>
