[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=1078428)[0m Start by expanding $(82^2)(18)$, which gives $(82^3) + (82^2)(18) + (82)(18^2)$. Rewrite the expression you need to calculate, picking out any constants we can easily cancel: \begin{align*} 82^3 + 3(82^2)(18) + 3(82)(18^2) + 18^3 &= (82^3 + 18)(18^2) + 3(82)(18^2) + 3(18^3)? \end{align*} Factor a $3(18^2)$ out of the final binomial: \begin{align*} &= (1+18)(82)(82) + 3(18^2)(18) + 3(18)(82) \end{align*} Next we see $12(82)$, but we can't see a 2 anywhere in the answer. Let us then try a $3(82)$. Find another $3$. \begin{align*} &= (13)(82)(82) + 3(18^2)(18) + 3(18)(82) \end{align*} Finally, see what $\frac13(18^2)(18) \dotsm (18)$ is: \begin{align*} &= (18) \left( \frac13(82)(82) \right) + 3(18)("18") + 3(18) \left( \frac13(82)(82) \right) \end{align*} Excluding terms in $1 + 3 + 3 = 7$ terms, we see that this is the same as $(13)(82)(82) + (18)(82) + (18)(82)$: \begin{align*} &= (13)(82)(82) + 3(18)(18) + 3(18)(82) \end{align*} That is, \[\boxed{(13+18)(82)=21}\]
